{{- /*
products.gohtml is the catalog list, optionally narrowed by a category, by a
search, or by both.

The rows come from the cross-module read layer, not from a module's service: the
panel knows no module (ADR 0011). Every field is printed as an ordinary value,
so the template engine's escaping applies to all of them — a product title, a
category name and the text an operator typed into the box are all operator-
supplied text, and the panel runs inside an administrator's session.

Paging shows "next" only when the read returned one record more than the page
holds. There is no total and no last page: a count over a growing catalog is the
expensive half of paging and this screen does not need it.

THE FILTERS TRAVEL IN THE ADDRESS, and the paging links carry them. A "next
page" that dropped the category or the search would walk the reader out of the
narrowed view and into a wider catalog without saying so — the same failure the
sales report's links avoid by carrying their period. They are appended
CONDITIONALLY here, unlike there: that report always covers some window, while
this list is unnarrowed most of the time, and "&category=&search=" hanging off
every link would be noise in an address bar operators read. If one did leak
through, the handler trims the value and an empty one is not a filter, so it
would be harmless rather than a narrowing by something nothing matches.

THE TWO CONTROLS ARE ONE FORM, which is what makes them compose. Two forms
would each submit only their own control, so choosing a category would discard
the typed search and searching would discard the category — silently, with a
200 and a wider list. Searching inside a category is the whole reason the box
was added, so both controls submit together.

THE FORM DOES NOT CARRY THE PAGE, on purpose. Choosing a category or typing a
search starts a new listing, and page 7 of the unfiltered catalog names nothing
in the narrowed one; submitting from there would land on an empty page of a
view that has plenty.

THE CONTROLS AND THE LIST MUST NEVER DISAGREE. Three states below say so out
loud for the category, because in each of them the dropdown alone would be
misleading: the vocabulary could not be read, it was read and does not contain
the chosen identifier, or it was cut short so the identifier could not be
checked. The search has no such states — there is no vocabulary behind a box —
so its whole visibility is the text staying in it and one line naming what is
being matched. The one thing this screen must not do is show an empty table
under a plain "Products" heading: an operator reads that as an empty catalog.

THE SEARCH BOX SITS OUTSIDE THE TEST THAT HIDES THE DROPDOWN, on purpose. The
dropdown disappears when the vocabulary cannot be read, and a box nested inside
that test would disappear with it — taking away a control that never depended
on that read in the first place.

WHEN THE DROPDOWN IS ABSENT and a category is still applied, the form carries
that category in a hidden field instead: without it, submitting a search
would drop a narrowing the operator cannot even see any more, and the way back
to the whole catalog is the link in the notice rather than an accident of
submitting.
*/ -}}
{{ define "body" }}
<h1>Products</h1>

<form method="get" action="{{ .Path }}">
  <label for="search">Search</label>
  {{- /*
  The box is refilled with what was applied, not left blank. A blank box over a
  narrowed list is the same lie the "All categories" dropdown would tell over a
  filtered one: the operator retypes what they already searched for, or reads
  the short list as the whole catalog. Emptying the box and submitting is the
  second way back to the full list, next to the link under the form — the
  handler treats an empty term as no filter at all.
  */}}
  <input type="search" id="search" name="search" value="{{ .SearchFilter.Term }}">
  {{ with .CategoryFilter }}
  {{- if .Options }}
  <label for="category">Category</label>
  <select id="category" name="category">
    {{- /*
    The empty value is the way back to the whole catalog, and it is empty rather
    than a word like "all" because the handler treats an empty parameter as "no
    filter" — one spelling for "unfiltered" instead of two.
    */ -}}
    <option value="">All categories</option>
    {{- range .Options }}
    <option value="{{ .ID }}"{{ if .Selected }} selected{{ end }}>{{ .Name }}</option>
    {{- end }}
  </select>
  {{- else if .Applied }}
  <input type="hidden" name="category" value="{{ .ID }}">
  {{- end }}
  {{ end }}
  <button type="submit">Show</button>
</form>

{{ with .CategoryFilter }}
{{ if .Unavailable }}
{{- /*
The dropdown is absent because the category list could not be read. The page
still shows the products, since they were read successfully and a screen that
died over a control it could not fill would cost the operator the catalog too.
*/ -}}
<p class="muted">
  The category list could not be read, so the filter is not available.
  {{ if .Applied }}
  The products below are still narrowed to the category
  <code>{{ .ID }}</code>, which cannot be named or changed here —
  <a href="{{ $.Path }}">show all products</a>.
  {{ end }}
</p>
{{ else if .Unknown }}
<p class="muted">
  No category is known by the identifier <code>{{ .ID }}</code>. The list below
  is still narrowed by it, so what is under this line is what that filter
  matched and not the catalog —
  <a href="{{ $.Path }}">show all products</a>.
</p>
{{ else if .Unverified }}
<p class="muted">
  The category list is capped and was cut short, so <code>{{ .ID }}</code>
  could not be checked against it — it may well exist. The list below is
  narrowed by that identifier either way —
  <a href="{{ $.Path }}">show all products</a>.
</p>
{{ else if .Applied }}
<p class="muted">
  Narrowed to <strong>{{ .Name }}</strong> —
  <a href="{{ $.Path }}">show all products</a>.
</p>
{{ end }}
{{ end }}

{{- /*
Every "show all products" link above is the bare path, so it clears the search
as well as the category. That is what the words say and it is why they can stay:
a link promising all products that left a search applied would be the same
confident-wrong screen the notices exist to prevent. The link below is the other
half of the pair — it clears only the search and KEEPS the category, because
"clear the search" is a promise about the search, and dropping a narrowing the
operator did not ask to drop is how a filtered screen becomes an unfiltered one
by accident.
*/ -}}
{{ if .SearchFilter.Applied }}
<p class="muted">
  Matching <strong>{{ .SearchFilter.Term }}</strong> —
  <a href="{{ .Path }}{{ if .CategoryFilter.Applied }}?category={{ .CategoryFilter.ID }}{{ end }}">clear the search</a>.
</p>
{{ end }}

{{ if .Products }}
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Title</th><th>Handle</th><th>Status</th><th>Updated</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
  {{ range .Products }}
    <tr>
      <td><a href="{{ $.Path }}/{{ .ID }}">{{ .Title }}</a></td>
      <td>{{ .Handle }}</td>
      <td>{{ .Status }}</td>
      <td>{{ if not .UpdatedAt.IsZero }}{{ .UpdatedAt.Format "2006-01-02 15:04" }}{{ end }}</td>
    </tr>
  {{ end }}
  </tbody>
</table>
{{ else }}
{{- /*
The empty message names what emptied the page, and there are FOUR of them
because two independent narrowings make four states. "No products on this page"
under a search is true and useless: it is the sentence an operator reads as "the
catalog is empty", and the one thing they would not then check is the control
that emptied it. The reverse mistake costs as much — blaming a category when the
search is what matched nothing sends the operator to fix a taxonomy that is
fine. Each state therefore gets its own sentence, and the search is asked FIRST
because it is the narrowing the operator just typed.
*/ -}}
{{ if .SearchFilter.Applied }}
{{ if .CategoryFilter.Applied }}
<p>Nothing matched that search in this category.</p>
{{ else }}
<p>Nothing matched that search.</p>
{{ end }}
{{ else if .CategoryFilter.Applied }}
<p>No products in this category on this page.</p>
{{ else }}
<p>No products on this page.</p>
{{ end }}
{{ end }}

<nav>
  {{ if .HasPrev }}<a href="{{ .Path }}?page={{ .PrevPage }}{{ if .CategoryFilter.Applied }}&amp;category={{ .CategoryFilter.ID }}{{ end }}{{ if .SearchFilter.Applied }}&amp;search={{ .SearchFilter.Term }}{{ end }}">Previous</a>{{ end }}
  <span>Page {{ .Page }}</span>
  {{ if .HasNext }}<a href="{{ .Path }}?page={{ .NextPage }}{{ if .CategoryFilter.Applied }}&amp;category={{ .CategoryFilter.ID }}{{ end }}{{ if .SearchFilter.Applied }}&amp;search={{ .SearchFilter.Term }}{{ end }}">Next</a>{{ end }}
</nav>
{{ end }}
